public” nodes have both a direct path and redundancy to help compensate for failures.↵↵In reality and normal use, every node can join and follow one of three node paths, so by actual design this is a “one core” network. However, the founders made this choice and the network was not designed around this reality. We therefore created two special paths for the nodes to provide redundancy. These are as follows: one is a redundancy path and this will be more thoroughly explained in the upcoming posts. The second node is not the route to the network, although a testnet used to run tests.↵↵For each node in the network, we have two redundancy nodes which are also called balancing nodes in the network. These are required to compensate for the nodes that can only provide one service, the router. The three nodes are not replicated because they are only providing the support for the router. In contrast, the additional redundancy nodes cost us nothing in the terms of space or bandwidth. Every node in the ZeroNet network can be one of the following:↵↵Main Path node—points in one of three directions and can be freely chosen.↵↵Mitancy node—points in one of two directions and can be freely chosen.↵↵
